<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Barcid family</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Barcid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Barcid family was a leading Carthaginian family of the third century BCE, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca">Hamilcar Barca</a> and his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>, Hasdrubal and Mago. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a> they built a power base in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, rich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines. Hannibal's invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> from there began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>, whose defeat brought the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/barcid-family.jpg" alt="Barcid family" width="320" height="325" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Ogre at en.wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hasdrubal_coin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the 3rd century BCE the Barcids were one of the leading families in the ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. Barcid is a form coined by historians, like Ramesside or Abbasid; the family's own byname was the Northwest Semitic Barca or Barcas, meaning lightning, a word related to barq in Arabic, berqa in Maltese and mebrek in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a>. The patriarch was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca">Hamilcar Barca</a>, who lived from 275 to 228 BCE. He and his wife, whose name is unknown, had three sons, who all became military leaders, and three daughters, who married allies of the family.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Hamilcar served as a general in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a>, from 264 to 241 BCE, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary-war">Mercenary War</a> that followed. He is said to have made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> swear on an altar never to be a friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. He then extended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>'s holdings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, where he drowned crossing a river. His son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-the-fair">Hasdrubal the Fair</a> succeeded to the command, extended the new province by diplomacy, founded Qart Hadast, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Carthago Nova</a> and now Cartagena, as its capital and fixed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> as the boundary with Rome by treaty, before he was killed by a Celtic assassin.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Battle of Cannae</a> in 216 BCE and lost at Zama in 202 BCE, and crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> with 60,000 soldiers and 38 elephants. His brother Hasdrubal defended the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> after Hannibal left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 218 BCE, and was defeated and killed at the Battle of the Metaurus in 207 BCE while bringing reinforcements. The youngest, Mago, fought in most of Hannibal's battles and often led the decisive push.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Barcids founded several Carthaginian cities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, some of which still exist, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahon">Mahón</a> and Cartagena, and their name is often given as the origin of the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>. One of Hamilcar's daughters married the Numidian chieftain Naravas; the name Salammbo sometimes given to her comes from the title of a novel by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustave-flaubert">Gustave Flaubert</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Barcids” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Silver from the Spanish mines, exploited intensively after the <b>Barcid</b> conquest of southern Iberia beginning in 237 BCE, financed Carthage’s late imperial power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Barcid</b> Iberia was run almost as a family dominion of the Barcids, nominally under Carthage.</blockquote><a href="/state/barcid-iberia">Barcid Iberia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-house"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Rulers and members</h2><figure class="pp-chart"><p class="cty-scale" aria-hidden="true"><span>300 BCE</span><span>225 BCE</span><span>150 BCE</span></p><ol class="cty-tl pp-tl"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/himilce"><img class="pp-av" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/himilce.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="28" height="28"><span class="pp-nm"><span class="cty-n">Himilce</span><span class="cty-y">3rd century BCE</span></span></a><span class="cty-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="cty-bar pp-dim" style="left:0.7%;width:66.0%"></span></span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca"><img class="pp-av" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hamilcar-barca.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="28" height="28"><span class="pp-nm"><span class="cty-n">Hamilcar Barca</span><span class="cty-y">c. 275–228 BCE</span></span></a><span class="cty-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="cty-bar pp-life" style="left:16.7%;width:31.3%"></span></span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-the-fair"><img class="pp-av" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hasdrubal-the-fair.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="28" height="28"><span class="pp-nm"><span class="cty-n">Hasdrubal the Fair</span><span class="cty-y">c. 270–221 BCE</span></span></a><span class="cty-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="cty-bar pp-life" style="left:20.0%;width:32.7%"></span></span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/hannibal"><img class="pp-av" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hannibal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="28" height="28"><span class="pp-nm"><span class="cty-n">Hannibal</span><span class="cty-y">247–c. 183 BCE</span></span></a><span class="cty-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="cty-bar pp-life" style="left:35.3%;width:42.7%"></span></span></li></ol><figcaption>4 people of the encyclopedia whose Wikidata record names this house as their family. Bright bars are lifespans; pale bars reigns or years of activity.</figcaption></figure><h3 class="ft-h" id="